    "body": "presented by\nDesigned for the global market, GA-ASI’s Gambit 6 UCAV is built to fly with crewed and uncrewed aircraft to secure the modern battlespace with precision air-to-ground strike capability. (Artist’s rendering)\nThe threats faced by crewed fighters on today’s battlefield have proliferated at a staggering pace. That’s why the time has come for the U.S., along with its allies in Europe and across the globe, to adopt autonomous Uncrewed Aircraft Systems (UAS) at scale.\nThe modern air defense environment has grown dramatically more lethal. Powerful radars, highly capable anti-air missiles, and loitering munitions now menace even the most advanced tactical aircraft. Ground-based, long-range missiles can threaten fixed targets, such as airfields, across entire regions.\nThese capabilities are no longer limited to major powers with advanced technology — they have trickled down to second-tier nations and even terrorist groups.\nThe reemergence of industrial-scale warfare demands an industrial-scale response. Yet the cost of still-vital crewed fighters has become unaffordable even to nations with world-leading defense budgets such as the United States.\nCrewed-uncrewed teaming offers a solution. By using uncrewed fighters designed for simplicity and mass production, nations can affordably overwhelm, disperse, and eliminate enemy air defenses while diverting enemy tactical aircraft away from crewed fighters and bombers.\nGA-ASI’s Gambit Series of Uncrewed Aerial Combat Vehicles (UCAVs) was developed to address precisely this challenge. Each Gambit is a mission-focused uncrewed combat aircraft designed to operate alongside crewed fighters.\nA Modular Approach to Affordable Mass\nRather than building a single aircraft for every mission, GA-ASI created a modular family of uncrewed jet fighters with variants optimized for distinct operational roles.\nThis approach avoids the costs of multi-mission aircraft, which in many cases are not best suited for roles that require flying into the teeth of enemy air defenses. It also retains common hardware across the family. Indeed, Gambit strikes the right balance between cost and capability.\nAbout 70 percent of Gambit’s key components, including the landing gear, baseline avionics, and core airframe structures, are shared among its variants. This commonality simplifies production, lowers lifecycle costs, and accelerates the creation of mission-specific aircraft.\nThe family includes:\nGambit 1 : Long-endurance ISR\nGambit 2 : Air-to-air combat\nGambit 3 : Adversary air training\nGambit 4 : Stealth combat reconnaissance\nGambit 5 : Carrier-based operations\nGambit 6 : Air-to-ground missions\nGambit 6 was developed specifically to meet the needs of European nations facing advanced, industrial-scale threats on their eastern front. It has been optimized for electronic warfare, suppression of enemy air defenses, and deep precision strike in contested environments.\nEach Gambit features a signature-reducing internal weapons bay that supports the integration of electronic warfare systems, precision-guided munitions, sensors, and autonomy software.\nThe modular architecture and signature-reducing weapons bay allow for easy integration of advanced autonomy, sensors, and weapons systems, ensuring the aircraft can adapt to a wide range of operational scenarios.\nOperational Experience and Production Readiness\nThe U.S. Air Force recently awarded an initial production contract to GA-ASI for its FQ-42 Vengeance Collaborative Combat Aircraft (CCA), a derivative of Gambit 2. This means that GA-ASI is already in serial production of the world’s first uncrewed fighter jet.\nThe FQ-42 Vengeance CCA is now in full-scale production for the U.S. Air Force.\nGA-ASI’s position in the CCA market is rooted in nearly two decades of development, both through independent research and development and in collaboration with the U.S. Air Force.\nLeveraging its more than five million square feet of fully operational manufacturing facilities, GA-ASI is ready to begin producing aircraft at scale the moment a contract is awarded.\nOptimized for European Requirements\nFor European customers, Gambit is a platform that supports sovereign integration and industrial participation.\nGeneral Atomics’ German affiliate, General Atomics AeroTec Systems GmbH, could support local assembly, integration, and testing for German programs, while partnerships with European industry would enable integration of national sensors, mission systems, and autonomy software.\nGA-ASI’s CCA solution for Europe leverages the U.S. Air Force’s FQ-42 and would be assembled in Germany in partnership with European industry.\nGA-ASI expects Gambit 6 airframes to be available for international procurement beginning in 2027, with European missionized versions deliverable by 2029.\nBy choosing Gambit, European nations gain a combat-ready, sovereign platform that combines cutting-edge technology with domestic industrial growth and strategic autonomy.\nWith its unmatched capabilities, production readiness, and commitment to indigenous integration, Gambit represents a scalable solution designed to counter today’s threats and address tomorrow’s challenges.",
